The clang of hammer on steel was Elara’s heartbeat. It resonated through her small workshop, a rhythmic pulse that had been a constant in her life since she was a child, perched on a stool watching her grandfather shape glowing metal into intricate hinges and sturdy tools. Now, years later, the workshop was hers, a haven filled with the scent of coal smoke, hot metal, and the faint, lingering aroma of beeswax used to polish her finished pieces.

Elara wasn't a blacksmith in the traditional sense. She didn't forge horseshoes or plowshares. Her passion lay in the delicate, the ornamental, the things that whispered of artistry rather than brute strength. She crafted intricate metal flowers that bloomed eternally, delicate filigree lanterns that cast dancing shadows, and whimsical creatures – tiny dragons with articulated wings, graceful stags with antlers of woven wire – that seemed poised to leap from their pedestals.

Her hands, calloused and strong, moved with a surprising grace. They knew the language of the metal, understood its resistance and its pliability. She could coax a stubborn bar of iron into a flowing curve, a sheet of copper into a shimmering petal, with a patience that bordered on reverence.  She didn't just work the metal;
